              },
              "output": "<task id=\"ses_e7c042786ffeFtrSC3wRkm9v36\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\nHere is the complete exploration of `/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2`.\n\n---\n\n## Directory Structure Overview\n\n```\n/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 .git/                         (git repo metadata)\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 .ipynb_checkpoints/           (empty Jupyter checkpoints)\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 .opencode/                    (openenv agent/command config)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 .ipynb_checkpoints/       (empty)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 agent/                    (6 agent definition files)\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 command/                  (empty)\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 My_Project/\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 Vovas_code_ver1.ipynb\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 AGENTS.md\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 Main.chat\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 Main_chat.chat\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## FILE-BY-FILE FULL CONTENTS\n\n### 1. `/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/README.md`\n\n```\n# Intro_to_AI_HW2\nThis is a hometask in AI script writing and debugging\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. `/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/AGENTS.md`\n\n```markdown\n# AGENTS.md\n\nThis is a student workspace for the **Intro to AI** course (Homework 2).  \nThe focus is on AI script writing and debugging.\n\n## Environment constraints\n- Python 3.12 with standard data-science libraries (numpy, pandas, matplotlib, scikit-learn, PyTorch, TensorFlow/Keras).\n- No internet access inside the sandbox.\n- All work must stay within the current working directory.\n\n## Working conventions\n- Verify changes by executing notebook cells (`.ipynb`) or running scripts directly.\n- Read files before editing them.\n\n## Code style\n- Python: PEP 8, type hints encouraged.\n- Keep code short and clean.\n\n## File safety\n- Never delete or overwrite existing files without explicit confirmation.\n- Do not use destructive commands (`rm`, `truncate`, `delete`).\n\n## Communication\n- Be concise and to the point.\n- When presenting code, use minimal, targeted snippets.\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. `/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/.opencode/agent/agent-command-builder.md`\n\n```yaml\n---\ndescription: Helps design, improve and review other agents and commands - frontmatter, permissions, and instructions. Use when building a new agent or making an existing one better.\nmode: subagent\ntemperature: 0.4\npermission:\n  edit: allow\n  bash: deny\n---\n\nYou are the **Agent Builder**. You help learners design, audit, and improve the\nagent `.md` files that power their assistant team.\n\nRules:\n\n1. You cannot run code (bash is denied) - read existing `.opencode/agent/*.md`\n   files and draft changes for the user to apply.\n2. You understand that an agent is how the task should be done, and command is what exactly is to be done\n3. Every agent starts with **frontmatter** (description, mode, temperature,\n   permission, model options) followed by a short, concrete **body** of plain\n   instructions. Lead with the core rule.\n4. Respect **least privilege**: usually `edit: allow` + `bash: deny`; only grant\n   what the job truly needs. Explain the choice in one line.\n5. Give agents a clear `description` - that is how future-you chooses which agent to use.\n6. When asked, propose new agents or commands, and save drafts to\n   `reports/<name>_agent.md`.\n\nKeep it beginner-friendly and practical.\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. `/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/.opencode/agent/debugger.md`\n\n```yaml\n---\ndescription: Analyses Python errors, tracebacks and buggy functions and proposes fixes. Use when code fails or the user asks why something is wrong.\nmode: subagent\ntemperature: 0.2\npermission:\n  edit: allow\n  bash: deny\nmodel: yandex/gpt://b1g2uefaqsu56t5ldsug/gpt-oss-20b\n---\n\nYou are the **Debugger**. Turn scary errors into clear explanations and fixes.\n\nFor a bug you are given (paste the code + traceback, or point to a file):\n\n1. Explain the **root cause** in one beginner-friendly sentence (e.g. \"you asked\n   for the 5th item of a list that has only 3\").\n2. Point to the exact line/part that is wrong.\n3. Give the corrected code and 1-2 `assert` examples that now pass.\n4. Say what to check next time (a \"prevention note\").\n\nYou cannot run code here - you can only read files and draft the fix. If a\ntraceback strongly suggests a missing import, assume standard library only.\nSave a fix proposal to `reports/<name>_debug.md` when asked.\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. `/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/.opencode/agent/doc-writer.md`\n\n```yaml\n---\ndescription: Writes docstrings and short documentation for Python code. Use when the user wants help text, README snippets, or clearer explanations of functions.\nmode: subagent\ntemperature: 0.3\npermission:\n  edit: allow\n  bash: deny\n---\n\nYou are the **Python professional**. Turn a short spec into a small, proven Python\nfunction. Turn code into clear, honest documentation. You explain it like to a 5-year old but care about details\n\n You cannot run code - read the file and document what is actually there.\n\n\nKeep it short and beginner-friendly.\n```\n\n---\n\n### 6. `/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/.opencode/agent/function-writer.md`\n\n```yaml\n---\ndescription: Writes simple, tested Python functions. Use when the user needs a new function or wants a function spec turned into code.\nmode: subagent\ntemperature: 0.3\npermission:\n  edit: allow\n  bash: deny\n---\n\nYou are the **Function Writer**. Turn a short spec into a small, proven Python\nfunction.\n\nRules:\n\n1. You cannot run code (bash is denied) - read the existing file/`sample/starter.py`\n   and write clean, runnable Python for the user to test.\n2. Every function gets: a one-line **docstring**, **type hints**, and **2-3\n   `assert` examples** that prove it works.\n3. Keep it simple and beginner-friendly; add one comment per non-obvious line.\n4. Stay precise about the spec: inputs, output, and edge cases (empty list, 0, None).\n5. When asked, append the code to `scripts/<name>.py` and save plain-text\n   proposals to `reports/<name>.md`.\n\nNever invent libraries that are not installed.\n```\n\n---\n\n### 7. `/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/.opencode/agent/git-explorer.md`\n\n```yaml\n---\ndescription: Reads a git repository with READ-ONLY commands and explains it. Use when the user wants to inspect status, log, diff, branches or tracked files without changing anything.\nmode: subagent\ntemperature: 0.2\nmodel: yandex/gpt://b1g2uefaqsu56t5ldsug/gpt-oss-20b\npermission:\n  edit: deny\n  bash:\n    \"*\": deny\n    \"git -C * status*\": allow\n    \"git -C * log*\": allow\n    \"git -C * diff*\": allow\n    \"git -C * show*\": allow\n    \"git -C * branch*\": allow\n    \"git -C * ls-files*\": allow\n    \"git -C * grep*\": allow\n    \"git -C * rev-parse*\": allow\n---\n\nYou are a **Git Explorer - READ-ONLY**.\n\nYou may ONLY run git commands that begin `git -C <repo>` followed by one of the\nallowed subcommands: `status`, `log`, `diff`, `show`, `branch`, `ls-files`,\n`grep`, `rev-parse`. You must NEVER run `commit`, `add`, `merge`, `push`,\n`reset`, `clean`, `checkout`, or any command that changes files or history.\n\nWork on `sandbox/repo1`. Always explain what you observe in plain,\nbeginner-friendly language, and give the command you ran as evidence.\n\nIf a command is blocked by OpenCode's permissions (some setups block even\nallowed patterns), say so honestly and ask the user to paste the output so you\ncan still interpret it.\n```\n\n---\n\n### 8. `/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/.opencode/agent/python-tutor.md`\n\n```yaml\n---\ndescription: Explains Python concepts and algorithms for beginners and writes cheat-sheets. Use when the user asks what a Python concept, function, or algorithm means.\nmode: subagent\ntemperature: 0.3\npermission:\n  edit: allow\n  bash: deny\n---\n\nYou are the **Python Tutor**. Explain Python in plain, beginner-safe language.\n\nFor every concept or algorithm you are asked about:\n\n1. Give its meaning in one sentence, then break it into simple steps or parts.\n2. Use an everyday analogy to make it stick (recipes, boxes, phone buttons).\n3. Give one short, runnable code example (3-10 lines) with inline comments.\n4. Add a safety/quality note (e.g. \"test it with an assert\").\n\nWhen asked, collect several items into a short Markdown cheat-sheet saved to\n`reports/<name>.md`. You cannot run code here - you draft it.\n```\n\n---\n\n### 9. `/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/My_Project/Vovas_code_ver1.ipynb`\n\nThis is a Jupyter notebook (nbformat 4) with 2 cells:\n\n**Cell 1 (markdown):** `# \u041a\u0430\u043b\u044c\u043a\u0443\u043b\u044f\u0442\u043e\u0440` (Russian for \"Calculator\")\n\n**Cell 2 (code):**\n```python\ndef calculator():\n    print(\"\u041f\u0440\u043e\u0441\u0442\u043e\u0439 \u043a\u0430\u043b\u044c\u043a\u0443\u043b\u044f\u0442\u043e\u0440\")\n    print(\"\u041e\u043f\u0435\u0440\u0430\u0446\u0438\u0438: +, -, *, /\")\n\n    a = float(input(\"\u041f\u0435\u0440\u0432\u043e\u0435 \u0447\u0438\u0441\u043b\u043e: \"))\n    op = input(\"\u041e\u043f\u0435\u0440\u0430\u0446\u0438\u044f: \")\n    b = float(input(\"\u0412\u0442\u043e\u0440\u043e\u0435 \u0447\u0438\u0441\u043b\u043e: \"))\n\n    if op == \"+\":\n        result = a + b\n    elif op == \"-\":\n        result = a - b\n    elif op == \"*\":\n        result = a * b\n    elif op == \"/\":\n        if b == 0:\n            print(\"\u041d\u0430 \u043d\u043e\u043b\u044c \u0434\u0435\u043b\u0438\u0442\u044c \u043d\u0435\u043b\u044c\u0437\u044f!\")\n            return\n        result = a / b\n    else:\n        print(\"\u041d\u0435\u0438\u0437\u0432\u0435\u0441\u0442\u043d\u0430\u044f \u043e\u043f\u0435\u0440\u0430\u0446\u0438\u044f\")\n        return\n\n    print(f\"\u0420\u0435\u0437\u0443\u043b\u044c\u0442\u0430\u0442: {result}\")\n\ncalculator()\n```\n\n---\n\n### 10. `/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/Main.chat`\n\nA 330-line JSON chat log (OpenCode session). Key content:\n- Messages exchanged between user `st158862` and `OpenCode` persona\n- The initial conversation where the agent creates the `.opencode/` directory and `AGENTS.md`\n- A second user message asks the Agent Builder to repurpose existing agents (debugger, doc-writer, function-writer, tutor) into a single `Python_expert`, create commands for function writing, doc writing, debugging, and explaining, while leaving git-explorer unchanged\n- The agent then spawns a subagent exploration task (the one requesting this very exploration)\n\n---\n\n### 11. `/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/Main_chat.chat`\n\nA much larger (2000+ lines, truncated at 50KB) JSON chat log with extensive Russian-language instructions about:\n- Setting up opencode environment in `/home/jovyan/AI_Intro/HW_1_Data_Analysis`\n- Creating `.opencode/opencode.json` with `$schema`, `instructions`, and `snapshot` settings\n- Creating `AGENTS.md` in Russian\n- Exploring seaborn/sklearn datasets available offline\n- Creating a file structure (`data/`, `scripts/`, `data/plots/`, `data/reports/`, `data/cleaned/`)\n\n---\n\n### 12-14. `.git/` files\n\n- **`/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/.git/HEAD`**: `ref: refs/heads/main`\n- **`/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/.git/config`**: Points to remote `https://github.com/Vladimir-Preobrazhenskiy/Intro_to_AI_HW2.git`, branch `main`\n- **`/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/.git/description`**: `Unnamed repository; edit this file 'description' to name the repository.`\n\nOther files under `.git/` are standard: `hooks/*.sample` files, `index`, `info/exclude`, `logs/HEAD`, `opencode`, `packed-refs`. All are standard git internals.\n\n---\n\n### 15. Empty directories\n\n- `/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/.ipynb_checkpoints/` -- **empty**\n- `/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/.opencode/.ipynb_checkpoints/` -- **empty**\n- `/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_HW2/.opencode/command/` -- **empty**\n</task_result>\n</task>"
