    {
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::OpenCodeAcpPersona",
      "type": "msg",
      "time": 1793216084.0555735,
      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false,
      "id": "205ad0fe-d2a8-4cde-9744-d74dbcea0e51",
      "body": "## Final structure\n\n```\n.opencode/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 agent/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Python_expert.md          \u2190 NEW \u2014 merged agent (debug + docs + functions)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 debugger.md               \u2190 old, still present\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 doc-writer.md             \u2190 old, still present\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 function-writer.md        \u2190 old, still present\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 agent-command-builder.md  \u2190 untouched\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 git-explorer.md           \u2190 untouched\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 python-tutor.md           \u2190 untouched\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 command/\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 function-writing.md       \u2190 NEW\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 doc-writing.md            \u2190 NEW\n    \u2514\u2500\u2500 debugging.md              \u2190 NEW\n```\n\n---\n\n## How agents and commands differ (beginner-friendly)\n\nThink of a **kitchen**:\n\n| Concept | Analogy | What it is |\n|---------|---------|------------|\n| **Agent** | The **chef** | A *persona* with skills, a mindset (temperature), and permissions (what tools they can use). Defines **HOW** work is done. |\n| **Command** | The **recipe card** | A *task description*. Defines **WHAT** job to do. Says \"here is the work order \u2014 go to this agent.\" |\n\n### Concrete example\n\n**Before** (old way \u2014 three narrow chefs):\n\n- `debugger.md` = a chef who *only* debugs. Can't write docs, can't write new functions.\n- `doc-writer.md` = a chef who *only* documents. Can't debug, can't write new functions.\n- `function-writer.md` = a chef who *only* writes functions. Can't debug, can't document.\n\nProblem: you needed three separate agents for tasks that all require the same Python knowledge.\n\n**After** (new way \u2014 one chef, three recipe cards):\n\n- **`Python_expert.md`** = one skilled Python chef. Knows all three jobs.\n- **`function-writing.md`** = recipe card that says \"Write a function.\" \u2192 routed to Python Expert.\n- **`doc-writing.md`** = recipe card that says \"Write docs.\" \u2192 routed to Python Expert.\n- **`debugging.md`** = recipe card that says \"Debug this.\" \u2192 routed to Python Expert.\n\n### Frontmatter tells you the difference at a glance\n\n**Agent** (`Python_expert.md`):\n```yaml\nmode: subagent\ntemperature: 0.3\npermission:\n  edit: allow\n  bash: deny\n```\n\u2192 Defines *who* works, *how* they think, and *what tools* they have.\n\n**Command** (`function-writing.md`):\n```yaml\nagent: Python_expert\n```\n\u2192 Just says *which agent* handles this task. No temperature, no permissions \u2014 the agent already has those.\n\n### Why this matters\n\n- You pick a **command** when you know exactly what you need (\"write a function that sorts a list\").\n- You pick an **agent** when you need a general capability (\"I need Python help\" \u2192 Python Expert decides whether to write, debug, or document).\n- Commands give you precision; agents give you flexibility.\n\n---\n\n> **Note:** The old agent files (`debugger.md`, `doc-writer.md`, `function-writer.md`) are still in the folder. They are superseded by `Python_expert.md` but not deleted. Do you want me to remove them, or keep them as reference?"
    },
